Parallel projection — no perspective foreshortening. Used for 2D / isometric / CAD / minimaps.
const camera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(
left, right, top, bottom, near, far
);
To keep things sized correctly with the window, derive from the aspect ratio:
const aspect = innerWidth / innerHeight;
const d = 10; // view height in world units
const camera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(
-d * aspect, d * aspect, d, -d, 0.1, 1000
);
On resize:
camera.left = -d * aspect;
camera.right = d * aspect;
camera.top = d;
camera.bottom = -d;
camera.updateProjectionMatrix();

camera.position.set(5, 5, 5);
camera.lookAt(0, 0, 0); // target in world space
lookAt orients the camera; it doesn't move it. Call after setting position.
To follow a moving target every frame:
renderer.setAnimationLoop(() => {
camera.lookAt(player.position);
renderer.render(scene, camera);
});
For smooth chase cameras, lerp position + lookAt:
camera.position.lerp(desiredPos, 0.1);
camera.lookAt(desiredLookAt);

import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
const controls = new OrbitControls(camera, renderer.domElement);
controls.enableDamping = true; // smooth inertia
controls.dampingFactor = 0.05;
controls.minDistance = 1;
controls.maxDistance = 50;
controls.maxPolarAngle = Math.PI * 0.49; // don't let user look from below ground
renderer.setAnimationLoop(() => {
controls.update(); // required when damping or autoRotate
renderer.render(scene, camera);
});
controls.target.set(x, y, z) repositions the orbit pivot. Useful to keep focus on a model whose centre is not at the origin.

import { PointerLockControls } from 'three/addons/controls/PointerLockControls.js';
const controls = new PointerLockControls(camera, document.body);
document.body.addEventListener('click', () => controls.lock());
On Escape, the browser unlocks the pointer automatically. Combine with custom WASD logic to walk.

To know what's under the mouse:
const raycaster = new THREE.Raycaster();
const pointer = new THREE.Vector2();
renderer.domElement.addEventListener('pointermove', (e) => {
pointer.x = (e.clientX / innerWidth) * 2 - 1;
pointer.y = -(e.clientY / innerHeight) * 2 + 1;
});
renderer.setAnimationLoop(() => {
raycaster.setFromCamera(pointer, camera);
const hits = raycaster.intersectObjects(scene.children, true); // recursive
if (hits.length) {
hits[0].object.material.color.set(0xff0000);
}
renderer.render(scene, camera);
});

A camera is an Object3D. You can add it to a group and the group's transforms cascade:
const rig = new THREE.Group();
rig.position.set(0, 1.6, 0); // head height
rig.add(camera);
scene.add(rig);
// move the rig — camera moves with it
rig.position.x += 0.1;
Useful for: FPS character rigs (move rig for body, rotate camera for look), AR camera mounts, cinematic camera tracks.

Render two views in one canvas (mini-map, picture-in-picture):
function render() {
// main view — full canvas
renderer.setViewport(0, 0, innerWidth, innerHeight);
renderer.setScissor(0, 0, innerWidth, innerHeight);
renderer.setScissorTest(true);
renderer.render(scene, mainCamera);
// mini-map — top-right corner 200×200
renderer.setViewport(innerWidth - 210, innerHeight - 210, 200, 200);
renderer.setScissor(innerWidth - 210, innerHeight - 210, 200, 200);
renderer.render(scene, mapCamera);
}
Each viewport gets its own camera but shares the scene.
